 "lead": {
  "label": "The Soothsayer",
  "kicker": "Franz Beard · Senior Columnist",
  "title": "Improved defense will carry Gators to 7th win",
  "url": "/post/the-soothsayer-improved-defense-will-carry-gators-to-7th-win",
  "html": "<blockquote><p>“Patience young Grasshopper” – Master Po to young Kwai Chang Caine from the 1970s television series “Kung Fu”</p></blockquote><p>When it comes to defense, patience was in rather short supply during Florida’s first six games of the season, particularly after a four-game stretch against Auburn, Ole Miss, Missouri and South Carolina. The Gators gave up a collective 150 points in those four games – two wins and two losses – an average of 438 total yards, 297 of which came through the air. Florida’s beleaguered secondary surrendered 10 touchdown passes while giving up 297 air yards.</p><p>A funny thing happened on the way to a defensive clone of 2020 when the Third and Grantham Gators gave up 428 yards and 30.8 points per game. Midway through the season, the same Brad White whom many in the Gator Nation wanted to see exiled to Akron or UMass began finding a formula that would work. In the four games since the South Carolina disaster, the Gators have held opponents to a very respectable 297.25 yards and 22 points per game.</p><p>After half a season of life with the defensive bottom feeders of the Southeastern Conference the Gators have moved up the ranks to 11th in the SEC and 64th nationally. To put it in better perspective, the Gators are giving up only 354.7 yards per game now, which, if it stands will be the best Florida defense since 2019.</p><p>The improvement has been mostly subtle but occasionally spectacular. What the Gators did last week especially in the in the fourth quarter turned Kentucky’s playoff dreams into a nightmare finish. The Gators gave up a 15-play, 86-yard touchdown drive on UK’s first possession then held the Wildcats to a short-field touchdown and just 156 yards after that.</p><p>On Kentucky’s last two possessions, the Gators got a fourth down stop when Aaron Chiles blitzed up the middle and planted Kenny Minchey just as he was throwing and a four-and-out that was all incompletions. That wouldn’t have happened prior to mid-October.</p><p>The key word here has been patience. Head ball coach Jon Sumrall trusted White to bring the defensive troops along. A couple of tweaks that included some fairly exotic blitz packages significantly shortened the time opposing QBs have to throw the ball, lessening the time the young corners have to cover.</p><p>It is a formula that has worked well and a reason why the Gators (6-4, 4-4 SEC) are favored by 13.5 points to take down Vanderbilt in the final home game of the season Saturday (12 noon, SEC Network). The Commodores don’t run the ball well at all (102 yards per game) and without the run threat to worry about the Gators should make life miserable for true freshman QB Jared Curtis.</p><p>The way The Soothsayer views this one, it won’t take a lot of points to send Vandy packing back to Nashville but it will take another strong effort out of a defense that has matured to secure a seventh win and assure the Gators of just their second regular season winning record since 2020.</p><p class=\"pg-sooth\"><strong>The Sayer Says Sooth!:</strong> Three touchdowns should get the job done for Florida which will move one step closer to an 8-win regular season.</p>",
  "continue": "Read the full column: Franz on every SEC game",
  "date": "Nov. 20, 2026"
 },
